fr:frère.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:frere}}
{{inh+|fr|fro|frere}}, from older {{m|fro|fredre}}, {{m|fro|fradre}}, from {{inh|fr|la|frāter|frātrem}}, from {{inh|fr|itc-pro|*frātēr}}, ultimately from {{inh|fr|ine-pro|*bʰréh₂tēr}}.

fr:chien.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|ine-pro:*ḱwṓ}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|chien}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|chien}}, from {{inh|fr|la|canis|canis, canem|t=dog}}.

See cognates in regional languages in France : {{cog|roa-brg|chein}}, {{cog|ca|ca}}, {{cog|co|cane}}, {{m|co|cani}}, {{cog|frp|shin}}, {{m|frp|tsin}}, {{cog|roa-gal|chien}}, {{m|roa-gal|cun}}, {{m|roa-gal|qhun}} {{cog|nrf|quien}}, {{m|nrf|tchian}}, {{cog|oc|can}}, {{m|oc|chin}}, {{cog|pcd|quien}}, {{m|pcd|tchien}}.

See cognates in [[Romance languages]] outside France : {{cog|rup|cãni}}, {{cog|gl|can}}, {{cog|it|cane}}, {{cog|lmo|can}}, {{cog|lld|cian}

fr:cheval.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:cheval}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|cheval}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|cheval}}, from {{inh|fr|la-lat|caballus||horse}}, from {{inh|fr|la|caballus||pack horse}}, of disputed origin.

fr:poisson.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|itc-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|poisson}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|poisson}}, {{m|fro|peisson}}, from an older form {{m|fro|peis}} with suffix {{af|fr|-on}}, from {{inh|fr|la|piscis|piscem}}, see below. Alternatively, but less likely, through a {{inh|fr|la-vul||*pisciō, pisciōnem}}.<ref>{{cite-book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DmqmAAAAIAAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false|title=A Primer of Romance Philology in the Form of a Syllabus|publisher=Stanford University Press|year=1934|first=Frederick|last=Anderson}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|url=https://books.g

fr:lapin.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:lapin}}
{{dercat|fr|la|xib|qsb-ibe}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|lapin}}, alteration of {{m|frm|lapereau}}, with change of suffix after {{m|frm|connin}}, a word it replaced.

fr:eau.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:eau}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|[[eau]], [[eaue]]}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|ewe}}, {{m|fro|euwe}}, {{m|fro|egua||water}}, from {{inh|fr|la|aqua||water}}, from {{inh|fr|ine-pro|*h₂ekʷeh₂|t=water, flowing water}}, related to {{cog|frk|*ahu|*ahu ~ ahwā|t=stream, water}}. See also {{cog|en|ea|t=watercourse, river}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
See also {{cog|roa-ang|ave}}, {{m|roa-ang|eive}}, {{m|roa-ang|ève}}, {{m|roa-ang|ieau}}, {{cog|an|augua}}, {{cog|rup|apã}}, {{cog|ast|agua}}, {{cog|roa-bbn|aïe}}, {{m|roa-bbn|aigue}}, {{cog|roa-brg|eâ}}, {{cog|gme-bur|eai}}, {{cog|ca|aigua}}, {{co

fr:café.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:bor|it:caffè<id:coffee>|id=coffee}}
{{bor+|fr|it|caffè}}, first appearing as the form ''caveé'',<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|cafe|etym=1}}</ref> from {{der|fr|ota|قهوه|tr=kahve}} ({{cog|tr|kahve}}), from {{der|fr|ar|قَهْوَة||coffee}}. {{doublet|fr|caoua}}.

fr:pomme.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:pomme<ety:inh<fro:pome>>}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|pomme}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|pome}}, from {{inh|fr|la|pōma}}, plural of {{m|la|pōmum}}, {{glossary|reanalysis|reanalyzed}} as a feminine singular, from {{inh|fr|itc-pro|*poomos}}, from {{inh|fr|ine-pro||*h₂po-h₁ém-os|taken off}}. Compare {{cog|en|pome}}.

fr:bleu.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{ety|fr|id=blue}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|bleu}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|bloe|blöe}}, {{m|fro|bleve}}, {{m|fro|blef}}, {{m|fro|bleu|t=blue}}, from {{inh|fr|la-lat|blāvus}}, from {{der|fr|frk|*blāu|t=blue}}, from {{der|fr|gem-pro|*blēwaz|t=blue}}, from {{der|fr|ine-pro|*bʰléh₁-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰlh₁-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰleh₁-|t=yellow, grey, blue}}, from {{der|fr|ine-pro|*bʰel-|t=to shine}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|blāo||blue}} ({{cog|de|blau}}), {{cog|nl|blauw||blue}}, {{cog|ang|blāw||blue}}. More at {{m|en|blue}}.

fr:deux.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:deux}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|deux}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|deus}}, from {{inh|fr|la|duōs}}, masculine accusative plural of {{m|la|duo}}, from {{inh|fr|itc-pro|*duō}}, from {{inh|fr|ine-pro|*dwóh₁}}.

fr:école.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:eschole}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|eschole}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|escole}}, an early borrowing or semi-learned word taken from {{bor|fr|la|schola}}, from {{der|fr|grc|σχολή}}.

fr:livre.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|fr|id=book|:inh|frm:livre<id:book>}}
{{etymid|fr|book}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|livre}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|livre}}, a {{slbor|nocap=1|fr|la|liber|librum}}. The strictly inherited form would be {{m|fr||*loivre}}. {{doublet|fr|liber}}.

fr:parler.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:parler}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|parler}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|parler}}, from {{inh|fr|la-eme|parabolo|parabolāre|t=to express}}, from {{der|fr|la|parabola|t=parable, comparison}}, from {{der|fr|grc|παραβολή}}.

fr:être.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|itc-pro|ine-pro|inh=2}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|-}} and {{inh|fr|fro|estre}} and {{m|fro|ester}}, respectively from {{inh|fr|la-eme|essere|pos=< {{m|la|sum|esse}} "be"}} and {{inh|fr|la|sto|stāre|t=stand}}. Conjugations reflect both but more so the former. The form {{m|fr|suis}} "(I) am" appears to reflect {{inh|fr|la-vul||*suiō}} < {{der|fr|la|sum}}. Compare {{m|fr|ai}} "(I) have", {{m|fr|sais}} "(I) know" < {{m|la-vul||*aiō}}, {{m|la-vul||*saiō}} < {{m|la|habeō}}, {{m|la|sapiō}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
See also {{cog|roa-ang|éter}}, {{m|roa-ang|étère}}, {{m|roa-ang|eteur}},

fr:faire.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:faire}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|faire}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|faire}}, from {{inh|fr|la|facio|facere}}. The past historic and imperfect subjunctive stem ''fi-'' (''fis'', ''fis'', ''fit''...) originated in the Latin form {{m|la|fēcī}}, with {{w|Metaphony (Romance languages)|metaphony}} (*/ˈfed͡zi/ > */ˈfit͡s/).

fr:matin.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|fr|ine-pro|*meh₂-|id=good}}
{{inh+|fr|la|mātūtīnus||of the morning}}, from {{w|Mater_Matuta|Matuta}}, Roman goddess of morning.

fr:aujourd'hui.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|id=today|:inh|frm:auiourd'huy<id:today>}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|auiourd'huy}}; {{univerbation|fr|au|jour|de|hui|nocap=1|lit=on the day of today}}; since {{m|fr|hui}} comes from {{der|fr|la|hodie}}.

fr:tôt.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|fr|ine-pro|*ters-}}
From {{uder|fr|fro|tost}}, from {{uder|fr|la-vul||tostum}}, from the neuter of {{uder|fr|la|tostus||toasted}}, later meaning "hotly, promptly" in Vulgar Latin. Cognate to {{cog|it|tosto}}, {{cog|frp|toût}}, {{cog|ca|tost}}, {{cog|oc|tost}}, {{cog|wa|toist}}, {{cog|lld|tosc}}.

fr:minute.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{ety|fr|:inh|fro:minute<ety:bor<la:minūtus<alt:minūta>>>}}
{{inh+|fr|fro|minute}}, borrowed from {{der|fr|la|minūtus|minūta}}. Compare {{doublet|fr|menu|notext=1}}, an inherited doublet.

fr:neige.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|itc-pro|ine-pro}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|nege}}, {{m|frm|naige}} (14th c.), {{deverbal|fr|neiger|t=to snow|nocap=1}}. {{glossary|Displaced}} related {{cog|fro|noif}}, from {{cog|la|nix|nix, nivem}}.

fr:froid.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:froit}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|froit}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|froit}}, {{m|fro|freid}}, inherited from {{inh|fr|la|frīgidus||cold}} (through a syncopated {{inh|fr|la-vul|-}} or {{inh|fr|la-lat|-}} form {{m|la|frigdus}}, {{m|la||fricdus}}, attested in the Appendix Probi, or {{m|la|fridus}}, on a Pompeian inscription). {{doublet|fr|frigide}}, a later borrowing.

fr:magasin.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|id=store|:inh|fro:maguesin<t:storeroom, storehouse><ety:bor<it:magazzino<unc:>>>|tree=1}}
{{inh+|fr|fro|maguesin||storeroom, storehouse}}, either from a {{w|Languages of France|regional language}} (compare {{der|fr|la-med|magazenum|nocat=1}} as found in a contract allowing Marseillais merchants to build storehouses in ports of the [[Maghreb]]) or from {{der|fr|it|magazzino}}, from {{der|fr|ar|مَخَازِن}}, plural of {{m|ar|مَخْزَن||storeroom, storehouse}},<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|etym=1}}</ref> from {{m|ar|خَزَنَ||to store, to stock, to lay up}}. {{etydate|1389}}. {{doublet|f

fr:inquiet.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:inquiet<ety:lbor<la:inquiētus>>}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|inquiet}} (16th c.), a borrowing from {{der|fr|la|inquiētus}}. Earlier was the verb {{m|fr|inquiéter}} (12th c.). {{surface analysis|fr|in-|quiet}}.

fr:bureau.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|id=desk|:inh|fro:burel}}
{{inh+|fr|fro|burel}}, diminutive of {{m|fro|*bure}} (compare {{cog|frm|bure||coarse woolen cloth}}, {{cog|fr|bourre||hair, fluff}}), from {{der|fr|la-lat|burra||wool, fluff, shaggy cloth, coarse fabric}}; akin to {{cog|grc|βερβέριον||shabby garment}}.

fr:pause.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:pause}}
From {{inh|fr|frm|pause}} (14th c. in the musical sense), from {{der|fr|la|pausa}}. The {{m+|frm|-}} form may be merely a relatinized spelling of {{der|fr|fro|pose}} (“moment, period of time”, 12th c., whence {{cog|nl|poos}}), itself an early borrowing (if not inheritance) from the same Latin noun; at any rate both forms cannot be separated entirely.

fr:train.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|id=vehicle|:inh|frm:train<id:vehicle><ety:inh<fro:train<id:delay><ety:der<fro:trainer<alt:traïner><id:drag>>>>>}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|train}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|train}}, from the verb {{m|fro|trahiner|t=to pull, drag}} (whence {{m|fr|traîner}}).

fr:avion.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:af|la:avis<id:bird>|-on<id:diminutive suffix>|id=aeroplane}}
{{coin|fr|Q313078}} for a patent application. From earlier ''Avion'' (1875), from {{af|fr|la:avis<t:bird>|-on}}, replacing earlier {{m|fr|aéroplane}} after WWI.<ref>{{R:GNV|avion, aéroplane|corpus=30|startyear=1860}}</ref>

fr:savoir.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|itc-pro|ine-pro|inh=2}}
{{inh+|fr|fro|savoir|savoir/eir}}, from {{inh|fr|la-vul|*sapēre}}, from {{inh|fr|la|sapio|sapĕre}}.
{{box-top|Comments}}
The now-obsolete spelling {{m|fr|sçavoir}} owes its ⟨ç⟩ to the {{m+|la|scio|scīre|t=to know}}, which was long assumed to be the etymon of the French verb.

The 1SG present indicative {{m|fr|sais}} comes from {{m+|fro|sai}} < {{m+|la-vul|-}} */ˈsajo/ ≠ {{m+|la-cla|sapiō}}. The inflections with ⟨ch⟩ reflect regular palatalization of an original [pj], as in {{m|fr|sache}} < {{m+|fro|sachet|sacheṭ}} < */ˈsa(p)t͡ʃat/ < */ˈsapjat

fr:aussi.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|frk}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|aussi}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|ossi}}, {{m|fro|ausi}}, {{m|fro|alsi|t=as, in the same way as}}, from {{inh|fr|la-vul||*ale si|t=other so, else so}}, from {{der|fr|la|alius|alid, aliud|t=other, another, any other, else}} + {{m|la|sīc|t=so, thus}}; probably a {{calque|fr|frk|*allswā|t=as, likewise, in the same way|nocap=1|nocat=1}}. Compare also similarly formed {{cog|fro|altresi|t=as, in the same way as, also, as well}} and {{cog|it|altresì}}.

fr:plutôt.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|fr|ine-pro|*ters-}}
From {{uder|fr|frm|plustost}}, see {{af|fr|plus|tôt}}. Compare {{cog|it|piuttosto}} and {{cog|ca|més aviat}}. Compare the etymology of {{m+|en|rather}}.

fr:société.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|fr|:inh|frm:societé<id:society>|id=society}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|societé}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|societé}}, borrowed from {{der|fr|la|societās|societātem|t=society}}, from {{m|la|socius}}. Compare the inherited Old French doublet {{m|fro|soisté}}.

fr:gouvernement.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:af|gouverner<t:to govern; to steer>|-ment<id:nominal>}}
From {{af|fr|gouverner|gloss1=to govern; to steer|-ment<id:nominal>}}.

fr:environnement.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:af|environner<t:to surround; to encircle>|-ment<id:nominal>}}
From {{af|fr|environner|t1=to surround; to encircle|-ment|id2=nominal}}.

fr:hôpital.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:inh|frm:hospital}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|hospital}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|hospital}} (12th c.), a borrowing from {{bor|fr|la-lat|hospitāle||hospital, guesthouse}}, from {{der|fr|la|hospitālis||hospitable}}. {{doublet|fr|hôtel}}.

fr:réussir.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:from|réussite}}
{{back-form|fr|réussite||success}}, a borrowing from {{bor|fr|it|riuscita||result, outcome; success}}, past participle of {{m|it|riuscire}}.

fr:ampleur.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:af|ample<pos:adjective>|-eur<id:abstract><pos:suffix forming feminine abstract nouns from adjectives>|tree=1}}
From {{af|fr|ample<pos:adjective>|-eur<id:abstract><pos:suffix forming feminine abstract nouns from adjectives>}}.

fr:efficace.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:bor|la:efficāx<alt:efficācem><t:efficacious, effectual, powerful, efficient>}}
{{bor+|fr|la|efficāx|efficācem|efficacious, effectual, powerful, efficient}}.

de:ja.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|id=yes|:inh|gmh:ja<id:yes><ety:inh<goh:ja<id:yes><ety:inh<gem-pro:*ja<id:yes>>>>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ja}}, from {{inh|de|goh|ja|ja, jā|yes}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*ja|t=yes}}. Cognate with {{cog|yi|יאָ}}.

de:Vater.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:vater|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|vater}}, from {{inh|de|goh|fater}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*fader}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*fadēr}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*ph₂tḗr}}. Germanic cognates include {{cog|nl|vader}}, {{cog|nds|Vader}}, {{cog|en|father}}, {{cog|da|fader}}, {{cog|got|𐍆𐌰𐌳𐌰𐍂}}.

de:Kind.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|id=child|:inh|gmh:kint}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|kint}}, from {{inh|de|goh|kind}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*kind}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*kindą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*kinþą}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*ǵenh₁-|t=to give birth}}.

de:Ente.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ente}}, confluence of (1.) the plural and oblique singular of {{m|gmh|ant}} and (2.) the {{inh|de|goh|-}} variant {{m|goh|enita}}, both from older {{m|goh|anut}}, from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*anad}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|eend}}.

The sense “canard” (mid-19th c.) is a {{cal|de|fr|canard|nocap=1}}, but also ties in with the expression {{m|de|blaue Enten}} (“lies, humbug”, literally “blue ducks”, since 15th c.).

The sense “Citroën 2CV” is shared with {{cog|nl|eend}}. {{etystub|de}}

de:weiß.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{ety|de|id=white|:inh|gmh:wīȥ<ety:inh<goh:wīȥ>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|wīȥ}}, from {{inh|de|goh|wīȥ}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hwīt}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*hwītaz}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*ḱweytos|t=bright; shine}}.

de:drei.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:drī|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|drī}}, from {{inh|de|goh|dri}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*þrīʀ}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*þrīz}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*tréyes}}. Compare {{cog|nl|drie}}, {{cog|en|three}}, {{cog|da|tre}}.

de:vier.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:vier<ety:inh<goh:fior>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|vier}}, from {{inh|de|goh|fior}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*feuwar}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*fedwōr}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*kʷetwṓr}}, the neuter form of {{m|ine-pro|*kʷetwóres}}. Compare {{cog|nl|vier}}, {{cog|en|four}}, {{cog|da|fire}}, {{cog|sv|fyra}}.

de:Buch.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{ety|de|id=book|:inh|gmh:buoch<ety:inh<goh:buoh>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|buoch}}, from {{inh|de|goh|buoh}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*bōk}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*bōks}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|book}}.

de:haben.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|:inh|gmh:hān<id:have>|id=have}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|han|haben}}, from {{inh|de|goh|habēn}} (akin to {{cog|osx|hebbian}}, {{cog|non|hafa}} ({{cog|sv|hava}}/{{m|sv|ha}}), {{cog|ofs|habba}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽}}, {{cog|ang|habban}}), from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*habbjan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*habjaną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*kap-<id:seize>||to grasp}}. Cognates include {{cog|bar|håbn}}, {{cog|yi|האָבן}}, {{cog|nl|hebben}}, {{cog|en|have}}, {{cog|da|have}}.

de:machen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:machen|tree=1}}
{{inh+|de|gmh|machen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|mahhōn}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*makōn}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*mag-|t=to knead, mix, make}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|maken}}, {{cog|nds|maken}}, {{cog|en|make}}.

de:Tag.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|de|id=day|:inh|gmh:tac|tree=1|text=++}}

In earlier legal documents since Middle High German, {{m|de||Tag}} was frequently used in the meaning of “a fixed day for meeting, debate, or appointment,” which was associated with words like {{m|de|Reichstag}}, {{m|de|Landtag}}, later {{m|de|Ratstag}}, and {{m|de|tagen||to hold a meeting}}, etc.

de:groß.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:grōȥ<ety:inh<goh:grōȥ>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|grōȥ}}, from {{inh|de|goh|grōȥ}}, akin to {{cog|osx|grōt}}, compare {{cog|nl|groot}}. More at {{cog|en|great}}.

de:gut.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro}}
{{inh+|de|gmh|guot}}, from {{inh|de|goh|guot}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*gōd}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*gōdaz}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*gʰedʰ-||to unite, be associated, suit}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate to {{cog|lb|gutt}}, {{cog|sli|gutt}}, {{cog|nl|goed}}, {{cog|fy|goed}}, {{cog|stq|goud}}, {{cog|en|good}}, {{cog|da|god}}, {{cog|no|god}} and {{cog|sv|god}}.
{{col-bottom}}

de:Zeit.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|id=time|:inh|gmh:zīt<id:time>|tree=1|text=++}}

Cognate with {{cog|nds|Tiet}}, {{m|nds|Tied}}, {{cog|nl|tijd}}, {{cog|en|tide}}, {{cog|da|tid}}, {{cog|no|tid}}, {{cog|sv|tid}}. Also cognate, more distantly, with {{cog|en|time}}.

de:Monat.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|mōnōt}}, {{m|gmh|mānōt|g=m|g2=n}}, from {{inh|de|goh|mānōd|g=m}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*mānōþ}}. See also {{m|de|Mond}}. Compare {{cog|osx|mānoth}}, {{cog|nl|maand}}, {{cog|en|month}}, {{cog|da|måned}}.

de:Minute.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:der|la-med:minūtus<alt:minūta>}}
15th c., from {{der|de|la-med|minūta||60th of an hour; note}}.

de:Wetter.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|wëter}}, from {{inh|de|goh|wëtar}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*wedrą}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro||*h₂wéh₁dʰrom}}, from {{suf|nocat=1|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-|t1=to blow; wind|*-dʰrom|pos2=instrument noun–deriving suffix}}. Compare {{cog|gsw|Wätter}}, {{cog|nds-de|Weder}}, {{cog|lb|Wieder}}, {{cog|nl|weer}}, {{cog|en|weather}}, {{cog|da|vejr}}.

de:Arbeit.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*h₃erbʰ-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|arbeit}}, from {{inh|de|goh|arbeit}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*arbaiþi}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*arbaidiz}}, ultimately from {{der|de|ine-pro|*h₃órbʰos|t=orphan, servant, slave}}, from which {{cog|en|orphan}} is also derived. Cognate with {{cog|yi|אַרבעט}}, {{cog|enm|arveth}}.

de:Aufgabe.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|id=task|:inh|gmh:ūfgābe<id:task>|:afeq|auf<id:up>|Gabe<id:gift>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ūfgābe}}, verbal noun of {{m|gmh|ūfgëben}}, whence modern {{m|de|aufgeben}}. {{surf|de|auf|t1=up|Gabe|t2=gift, giving}}.

de:Zimmer.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:zimber<ety:inh<goh:zimbar<ety:inh<gmw-pro:*timr>>>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|zimber}}, from {{inh|de|goh|zimbar}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*timr}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*timrą}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*dem-|t=build, house}} (see {{cog|ine-pro|*dṓm}}). Cognate with {{cog|en|timber}}, {{cog|sv|timmer||timber}}, and {{cog|nl|timmer|t=building; construction; room}}.

de:Stadt.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:stat<ety:inh<goh:stat>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|stat}}, from {{inh|de|goh|stat}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*stadi}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*stadiz}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*stéh₂tis}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|stead}}. {{doublet|de|Statt|Stätte}}.

de:Straße.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*sterh₃-}}
{{etymon|de|:inh|gmh:strāȥe<ety:inh<goh:strāȥa<ety:inh<gmw-pro:*strātu>>>>|tree=1}}
{{inh+|de|gmh|strāȥe}}, from {{inh|de|goh|strāȥa}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*strātu|t=street}}, from {{der|de|la|([[via]]) [[strata]]}}.

{{senseno|de|strait|t=strait|cap=1}} is a {{cal|de|gml|strâte|nocap=1}},<!-- 14th c. --> itself a {{psm|de|enm|streit|nocap=1}}, from {{der|de|fro|estreit|t=narrow}}, from unrelated {{der|de|la|strictus}}.

de:lernen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|:inh|gmh:lernen<ety:inh<goh:lernēn>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|lernen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|lernēn}}, {{m|goh|lirnēn}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*liʀnēn}}. Cognate with {{cog|ang|leornian}} (Modern English {{m|en|learn}}). Related to {{m|de|lehren}}.

de:helfen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|:inh|gmh:hëlfen|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|helfen|hëlfen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|hëlfan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*helpan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*helpaną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|[[*ḱelb-]], [[*ḱelp-]]}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|helpen}}, {{cog|nl|helpen}}, {{cog|en|help}}, {{cog|da|hjælpe}}, {{cog|nb|hjelpe}}.

de:Gesetz.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|id=law|:inh|gmh:gesetze<ety:inh<goh:gisezzida<ety:from<sezzen<ety:inh<gmw-pro:*sattjan>>>>>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|gesetze}}, {{m|gmh|gesetzede}}, from {{inh|de|goh|gisezzida||fixing, determination, assessment}}, from {{m|goh|sezzen||to make sit, set, put}}, from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*sattjan||to set, put down}}.

de:Forschung.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:vorschunge<ety:inh<goh:forskunga<ety:inh<gmw-pro:*forskungu>>>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|vorschunge}}, from {{inh|de|goh|forskunga}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*forskungu}}, equivalent to {{suf|de|forschen|-ung||t1=to research}}.

de:heilen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|heilen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|heilen}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hailijan||to heal, make whole, save}}. See there for more cognates.

de:gelingen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|gelingen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|gilingan}}, a prefixed form of {{m|goh|lingan}}, from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*lingwan}}. Compare {{cog|yi|געלינגען}}.

de:schaffen.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|schaffen}} (strong verb), from {{inh|de|goh|scafan}}, {{m|goh|scaffen}}, byform of {{m|goh|skephen}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*skappjan}}. The form with ''-f-'' was chiefly backformed from the past participle {{m|goh||giscaffan}}, but was also influenced by the regular present conjugations {{m|de||du skeffis, er skeffit}}, and by the weak verb in etymology 2 below. The form {{m|goh||skephen}} survives in the (now archaic) variant {{m|de|schöpfen}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|scheppen}}, {{cog|en|shape}}.

de:wichtig.raw:
===Etymology===
From the same root as {{m|de|Gewicht||weight}} + {{m|de|-ig||-y}}, thus pertaining to {{m|de|wiegen||to weigh}}. See also {{m|de|gewichtig}}, a (formal or elevated) synonym of ''wichtig''. Weight is often used as a metaphor for importance;
compare {{cog|de|schwerwiegend||serious, grave}}, as well as {{cog|en|weighty}}, {{m|en|heavy}}.

de:Wert.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:wert}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|wert}}, from {{inh|de|goh|werd}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*werþaz}}. The ''-t'' is generalised from the uninflected form, likely reinforced by the desire to distinguish from forms of {{m|de|werden}} (compare {{m|de|werten}}). The regular ''-d-'' remains in {{m|de|Würde}}, {{m|de|würdig}}. Compare {{cog|nl|waard}}, {{cog|en|worth}}, {{cog|sv|värd}}.

de:nachhaltig.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:af|nachhalten<t:to last, to keep>|-ig}}
From {{af|de|nachhalten|-ig|t1=to last, to keep}}.

fr:dépouillement.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:af|dépouiller<t:to skin; to shed>|-ment<id:nominal>}}
From {{af|fr|dépouiller|t1=to skin; to shed|-ment|id2=nominal}}.

fr:cheminement.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|fr|:af|cheminer<t:to walk on; to keep walking>|-ment<id:nominal>}}
From {{af|fr|cheminer|t1=to walk on; to keep walking|-ment|id2=nominal}}.

fr:guère.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|fr|gem-pro}}
{{inh+|fr|frm|guere|guere(s)}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|gaire|gaire(s), guaire(s)|t=much}}, from {{der|fr|frk|*waigaro||much}}, related to {{m|gmw-pro|*waigr|t=resistant}}. Compare {{cog|gmh|unweiger|t=not much}}, which corresponds closely to the French construct. Romance cognates are {{cog|oc|-}} and {{cog|ca|gaire}}, {{cog|it|guari}}.

de:vertuschen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:inh|gmh:vertuschen<ety:af<gmh:ver-><gmh:tuschen>>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|vertuschen}}, {{m|gmh|vertussen}}, {{m|gmh|vertuzzen|t=to cover up, hide}}, from {{m|gmh|tuschen||to keep quiet, hide something}}, further origin {{unk|de|title=obscure}}. Apparently related to {{cog|gml|vörtussen|t=to hide, cover up, suppress}}. Compare also {{m+|goh|firtuskōn||to confuse, mislead}}. Influenced in meaning by false association with unrelated {{m|de|tuschen|t=to paint, use mascara}}.

de:entbehren.raw:
===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|enbërn}}, from {{inh|de|goh|inbëran}}. Equivalent to the prefix {{prefix|de|ent|*behren}}, an obsolete verb ultimately from the root of {{m|de|gebären|t=to give birth}}. Cognate with {{cog|lb|entbieren}}, {{cog|nl|ontberen}}.

de:übertragen.raw:
===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|de|id=to transfer|:calque|la:trānsferō<id:to transfer>}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|übertragen}}, at least in part a {{cal|de|la|transfero|transferre|nocap=1}}. Analysable as {{prefix|de|über|t1=over|tragen|t2=to carry}}. Compare {{cog|nl|overdragen}}, {{m+|en|carry over}}.

de:Umstand.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|la}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|umbestand|lit=a standing around; an [[um-#English|umb]]-stand}}, a Germanic {{calque|gmh|la|nocap=1|circumstans|nocat=1}} (whence {{ncog|en|circumstance}}), which is {{m|la|circum||about}} + {{m|la|stans||standing}}. {{surf|de|um-|Stand}}.

de:Rahmen.raw:
===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ram}}, {{m|gmh|rame|t=column, frame|g=f}}, from {{inh|de|goh|rama|t=support|g=f}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hramu|t=frame, support}}. Cognate with {{cog|hrx|Raume}}, {{cog|nl|raam|g=n}}.

de:Ausnahme.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:deverbal|ausnehmen<t:to exempt, to except>}}
{{deverbal|de|ausnehmen||to exempt, to except}}.

de:oberflächlich.raw:
===Etymology===
{{suffix|de|Oberfläche|lich}}

Could be a {{calque|de|la|superficiālis|nocap=1}}. Attested since the 18th century.

de:während.raw:
===Etymology===
{{ety|de|:from|währen}}
From the present participle of {{m|de|währen||to last, persist}}, with {{glossary|rebracketing}} of ''währendes [noun], währender [noun], währendem [noun]'' as ''während des [noun], während der [noun], während dem [noun]''.
